\title{OPT-K: the hybrid single entry - one executable that can hand a run to a JVM; one cold run of a corpus gains nothing from it up to Mathlib, twelve heavy small tests do}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{platform}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Work "OPT-K" of 2026-11-25, for a decision of the maintainer of that day: RLVM is to be shipped as a hybrid single entry, one executable \code{rlvm} that checks small files itself and hands large files to a JVM when a JDK 25 is found. Today the release is one executable, a GraalVM Community Edition native image ([packaging](design-packaging), [the settings of a plain run](dec-EGMG)); the JVM launcher \code{rlvm-jvm} is a tool of the repository that starts the same jars on a JVM ([the launchers](design-launchers)), since that day on a stock OpenJDK 25 as well ([the stock-JDK report](rep-OPT-J)). In the warm metric, the later passes of one process, the JVM is about 16 \% faster than the executable ([the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks)); a user's single run is a cold run, and for one cold run the executable was as fast or faster in every earlier table. This report measures first when handing over pays: the whole process as a user has it, from its start to its exit status, one cold run each. Then it designs the hybrid, builds a prototype in a private copy of the tree, runs the gates through it in each of its modes, and gives a verdict on making the automatic mode the default. No tracked file of the main tree was changed, nothing was installed into the shared repository of jars, nothing was published, and nothing of Oracle GraalVM was used.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ol{
\li{\strong{One cold run of a corpus gains nothing from the hand-over, up to Mathlib} (section 2). The whole process, start to exit status, 8 workers, five interleaved rounds per corpus on the published build: with the JVM launcher's flags on a stock OpenJDK 25 a JVM needs 2.4 times the executable's wall clock in the geometric mean of the eight corpora - 4.9 times on Init (3.24 against 0.66 s), 1.57 on cslib (8.05 against 5.13 s) and \strong{1.12 on Mathlib (16.33 against 14.56 s)}. A JVM pays about three seconds of warm-up on every file from Std to cslib and begins to earn them back only at Mathlib's size.}
\li{\strong{The best that shortens the warm-up is the AOT cache of JDK 25, and it ends 2 \% behind on Mathlib} (section 2.3): recorded once per JDK on one pass of Init in 8 s, 18.7 MB, it brings a cold JVM to 1.72 times the executable in the geometric mean and to \strong{1.02 on Mathlib (14.87 against 14.56 s)}, 1.03 in a second window three hours later. C1 alone is the best on everything short (1.25 to 1.56 times) and the worst on Mathlib. More compilation, fewer compiler threads, class-data sharing of RLVM's classes, a pre-touched or pre-committed heap: no gain or a loss.}
\li{\strong{For an export that keeps eight workers busy the crossover lies above Mathlib}, the largest export there is (6.33 GB). Extrapolated from the three largest corpora, a JVM with the launcher's flags would be level with the executable at about 30 s of the executable's time, twice Mathlib, an export of about 13 GB; with the AOT cache at about 18 s, 8 GB. This is a line through three points and not a measurement (section 2.4).}
\li{\strong{What does gain in a single run is a check that is long and leaves cores idle} (section 2.7): twelve of the arena's 262 small tests, exports of 25 kB to 4.6 MB whose check takes 1.5 to 9 s on one worker, are 10 to 47 \% faster on a JVM, which compiles on the seven idle cores; every small test under a second is slower, 1.4 to 12 times. Five interleaved rounds on six of them: 0.65 to 0.81 times the executable's wall clock from 1.5 s of work on, 1.59 times at 0.8 s. Neither the size of a file nor its number of units tells the two kinds apart before the check.}
\li{\strong{With several passes the JVM leads from the third pass on, and as a whole process only on Mathlib} (section 2.5): three passes in one process take 39.3 s on a JVM and 43.1 s in the executable on Mathlib (0.91), and 1.12 to 2.67 times the executable's wall clock on the other seven corpora. Three passes are not yet a JVM's steady state.}
\li{\strong{The hand-over itself costs 3 ms} (section 2.6): 48 microseconds to find a JDK from its \code{release} file, 0.8 ms to compare the 13 jars of the cache with the executable's copy, one \code{execv}; the first hand-over of a build on a machine costs 57 ms more, for unpacking (1.6 ms) and one probe of the JDK (55 ms). A tutorial test takes 4.5 ms in the executable and 93 ms on a JVM.}
\li{\strong{The design} (section 3): the 13 RLVM jars, 1.1 MB, are inside the executable; it unpacks them into a per-user cache directory, named after the build id, compares every jar with its own copy, byte for byte, on every start, and refuses a directory that anybody else could write or replace; it finds a JDK 25 or newer as \code{RLVM_JAVA}, \code{JAVA_HOME} or \code{java} on \code{PATH} and judges it by its \code{release} file, without starting it; it probes a JDK once, and then replaces itself by \code{java} with \code{execv}, so the exit status, with \code{RLVM_STATUS_BASE}, is the JVM side's own. Whatever fails before \code{execv} leaves the run to the executable. The option is \code{\verb~|--platform image|jvm|auto~} (\code{RLVM_PLATFORM}).}
\li{\strong{The rule of \code{auto}}, as the measurement leaves it: a single pass is never handed over, whatever the size, and a run with \code{\verb~|-X passes=N~}, N above 1, is. There is no size threshold: no corpus gains at any size we can test, and the small tests that gain cannot be told by their size.}
\li{\strong{The prototype works and the gates pass in every mode} (sections 4 and 5): five patches, one changed jar; the first four are the hybrid as measured, the fifth makes the hand-over an option. The gate of the default configuration through the hybrid executable under \code{image}, \code{auto} and \code{jvm} and through its jars: 230 of 230 published tests, 17,775 mutants and 1,274 hostile exports with no wrong accept and no wrong reject in each; forced to the JVM, all 19,279 files of the gate were checked by a JVM. The arena's 270 tests by the arena's own procedure, six times - the published executable, and the hybrid with nothing said, kept in the image and forced to the JVM, with a stock JDK 25 and with none: the same status, exit code and output on 270 of 270 every time. In the arena's metric a hand-over of everything lowers the Mathlib count by 6 \% (1,347 against 1,433 G instructions) and multiplies the count of the median small test by 184; under \code{auto} every count is the executable's. One-worker runs of the eight corpora through five launchers: 6,888 values compared, none different.}
\li{\strong{The guard} (section 6): under \code{auto} no corpus is slower in a single cold run than the executable alone: the same instructions, and 0.999 times the wall clock in the geometric mean (0.970 to 1.023; eight rounds in random order, in which two builds of the same sources differ by 0.992 to 1.020); a tutorial test takes the same 4.5 ms and has 0.9 MiB more resident (22.0 against 21.1 MiB: the jars in the image heap); with three passes the hybrid is the JVM launcher (Mathlib 0.985 in six rounds).}
\li{\strong{Verdict}: \strong{NO-GO for \code{auto} as the default, GO for the hybrid as an option.} The guard's second item holds for the time of a small input and not for its memory, by 0.9 MiB; and what \code{auto} would do as the default is to move runs of \code{\verb~|-X passes~} to a JVM, at the price that every tool which times the executable with several passes must then name the image. There is no threshold to give: no corpus gains at any size, and the small tests that gain cannot be told from their size. A fifth patch makes the executable the default and the hand-over an option (section 6.4).}
\li{\strong{Measured on} the aarch64 development host while another wave of the team loaded the other cores, with Eclipse Temurin 25.0.5.1+2 as the JDK. Section 8 has what was not measured.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was measured, and how}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The build}: the distribution published on 2026-11-25 at 20:00 UTC (jars sha256 \code{f5517aa135d2}, the executable 68,421,752 bytes), in a private copy whose 25 files have the sha256 of the shared one. Part 1 times this copy and nothing else: its executable \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~} and its JVM launcher \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~}.}
\li{\strong{The JDK} of every JVM row: Eclipse Temurin 25.0.5.1+2 for linux-aarch64, the stock OpenJDK 25 of [the stock-JDK report](rep-OPT-J), given to the launcher as \code{RLVM_JAVA}. The launcher's own flag line starts on it since that day.}
\li{\strong{A cell is ONE COLD PROCESS, timed from outside}: the monotonic clock of the parent from just before \code{fork} to the return of \code{wait4}, so the start of the runtime, the check and the exit are all in it; CPU time and peak resident set are the child's \code{rusage}. Every process is \code{\verb~|--threads 8 --stats FILE~} and nothing else: the default configuration, the machine with its default switch set, the file checked while it is read. The export is read once before the rounds of a corpus, so that every process finds it in the page cache: "cold" is the process, not the disk.}
\li{\strong{Rounds}: at least five per corpus; in every round each variant runs one process, and the order rotates from round to round. Everything that is compared ran inside one \src{bench/quiet.py} window on the eight measurement cores, behind \src{bench/slot.sh}, one process at a time ([the measurement method](design-measurement-method), [interleaved rounds](ins-MBQS)).}
\li{\strong{The kernel's state} is recorded for every process: the free blocks of 2 MiB and more before it, and the growth of \code{compact_stall}, \code{thp_fault_alloc} and \code{thp_fault_fallback} of \code{\verb~|/proc/vmstat~} while it ran (counters of the whole host). The JVM's line has \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseTransparentHugePages~}, which stalls when the host has no free huge pages ([the huge-page report](rep-OPT-G)). A round in which any process saw a compaction stall or a huge-page fallback is \strong{disturbed}: it is left out of the cells and one more round is run.}
\li{\strong{A cell} is the mean over the rounds, in brackets the smallest and the largest round; a ratio is the ratio of two cells; "round by round" are the ratios of the two processes of each round.}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.1 The windows, the host beside them and the kernel's state}

