\ul{
\li{\strong{Compaction stalls occurred in three windows, always on Mathlib}, the corpus whose JVM runs touch 5 GB under the huge-page advice: when the free 2 MiB blocks of the host fell to between 1,400 and 3,300, a JVM process saw tens to thousands of stalls and up to 1,029 fallbacks. Every such round was left out of the cells and run again; the tables say how many rounds a cell has.}
\li{The window of the small inputs (section 2.6) and the window of the heavy small tests (section 2.7) had no stall; the counters of the host did not move between their start and their end.}
\li{\strong{The load beside the windows}: 30 to 48 while the cold-run table was taken, 29 to 60 during the three-pass window, 9 to 20 for the heavy small tests, 26 to 36 for the last two windows.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. Part 1: one cold run of the executable against one cold run of a JVM}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 The table: (a) the executable, (b) the JVM launcher, (c) the best that shortens the JVM's warm-up}

\p{Wall clock of the whole process in seconds, 8 workers, five rounds per corpus, one window of 1,302 s on 2026-11-25 from 22:25 to 22:47 UTC, 280 processes, every one accepted with status 0, no compaction stall on the host and no huge-page fallback, so no round was left out. The corpora are in the order of the executable's time. (b) is \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~} on Temurin 25 with the launcher's own flags and heap; the two columns (c) are the same launcher with one thing added: the JDK 25 AOT cache recorded on one pass of Init (section 2.3), and \code{\verb~|-XX:TieredStopAtLevel=1~}.}

