}

\ul{
\li{\strong{No corpus gains from the hand-over in a single cold run.} With the launcher's flags a JVM needs 2.4 times the executable's wall clock in the geometric mean: 4.9 times on Init, 2.9 to 3.6 times on Std, cedar and con-leche, 2.6 times on reachability, 1.75 and 1.57 times on navier-stokes-euler and cslib, and 1.12 times on Mathlib (16.33 against 14.56 s). All 40 round ratios are above 1; the smallest is 1.11.}
\li{\strong{The difference in seconds is nearly constant up to cslib and shrinks only at Mathlib}: +2.6 s (Init), +3.0 (Std), +2.7 (con-leche), +3.0 (cedar), +2.9 (navier-stokes-euler), +2.9 (cslib), +1.8 (Mathlib); reachability, a short file, +0.8. A JVM pays about three seconds of warm-up whatever the file, and earns them back at 0.11 s per second of the executable's time only once the file is large.}
\li{\strong{The AOT cache is the best single thing for a large file and is still behind}: 1.72 times in the geometric mean, 2.5 times on Init, 1.28 on cslib, and \strong{1.02 on Mathlib}: 14.87 against 14.56 s, 0.31 s behind, with four of five rounds above 1 and one at 0.97. That is as close as any JVM configuration came to the executable on any corpus.}
\li{\strong{C1 alone is the best for everything short and never catches up}: 1.25 to 1.56 times on every corpus (Init 1.02 against 0.66 s). Without C2 the JVM has almost no warm-up to pay, and its steady state is 1.23 times slower than the executable's, so the difference grows with the file: +0.4 s on Init, +3.6 s on Mathlib.}
\li{\strong{The absolute times are those of a loaded host.} Another job of the team kept the twelve cores outside the window busy (a load of 30 to 48 throughout), and the executable's cold Mathlib run took 14.56 s where the benchmark row of the same build on a quiet host, taken the same evening, has 12.77 s; the JVM launcher's first pass there is 14.21 s. The ratio on the quiet host, 1.11, is the one measured here, 1.12.}
}

\p{\strong{The two largest corpora again}, three hours later, the same three processes, five rounds with the order drawn anew in every round (316 s, 01:20 to 01:26 UTC, the same evening; a load of 26 to 36 beside the window, no compaction stall):}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{export, MB}\th{units}\th{rounds}\th{(a) the executable, s}\th{(b) JVM launcher, s}\th{/ (a)}\th{round by round}\th{(c) with the AOT cache, s}\th{/ (a)}\th{round by round}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{2,501}\td{401,985}\td{5 of 5}\td{4.98 (4.94 - 5.01)}\td{7.72 (7.13 - 8.30)}\td{\strong{1.551}}\td{1.54 1.52 1.66 1.44 1.59}\td{6.39 (6.30 - 6.53)}\td{\strong{1.285}}\td{1.28 1.32 1.27 1.27 1.28}}
\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{6,326}\td{721,845}\td{5 of 5}\td{13.99 (13.59 - 14.25)}\td{15.59 (15.07 - 15.96)}\td{\strong{1.115}}\td{1.12 1.12 1.11 1.14 1.09}\td{14.38 (14.10 - 14.62)}\td{\strong{1.028}}\td{1.03 1.02 1.03 1.04 1.02}}
\tr{\td{\strong{geometric mean}}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{\strong{1.315}}\td{1.115 to 1.551}\td{}\td{\strong{1.149}}\td{1.028 to 1.285}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The same ratios.} The JVM launcher needs 1.55 times the executable's wall clock on cslib and 1.115 times on Mathlib (15.59 against 13.99 s); with the AOT cache 1.285 and 1.028 times (14.38 against 13.99 s). Over the two windows all ten Mathlib rounds have the plain JVM behind, by 9 to 14 \%, and nine of ten have the JVM with the cache behind, by 2 to 4 \%.}
}
}

