\li{\strong{A JVM costs about 90 ms around the check, whatever the file}; with the AOT cache 65 ms. The hybrid executable that hands over costs the same 85 to 94 ms as the JVM launcher's shell script: the hand-over itself is not visible at this scale (section 2.6 has it in microseconds).}
\li{\strong{The executable costs 33 ms on Init and 305 ms on Mathlib}: it grows with the memory the process gives back when it ends.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.3 What can shorten the JVM's warm-up: two screening windows}

\p{Before the table of 2.1, two windows of three rounds each on Init, cedar, cslib and Mathlib screened what does not change what is computed: 21:49 to 22:10 UTC (962 s, 161 processes) and 22:13 to 22:25 UTC (744 s, 108 processes). Seconds of the whole process, in brackets the ratio to the executable in the same rounds. In the first window one Mathlib process ran while the host compacted memory (47 stalls; the free 2 MiB blocks had fallen from 8,800 to 2,700): its round is left out and a fourth was run.}

