\table{
\tr{\th{variant}\th{init, s (/ executable)}\th{cedar, s (/ executable)}\th{cslib, s (/ executable)}\th{mathlib, s (/ executable)}}
\tr{\td{the executable}\td{0.65}\td{1.54}\td{4.99}\td{14.23}}
\tr{\td{JVM launcher, its own line}\td{3.34 (5.15)}\td{4.83 (3.14)}\td{7.72 (1.55)}\td{15.84 (1.11)}}
\tr{\td{AOT cache recorded on one pass of Init}\td{1.65 (2.54)}\td{3.06 (1.99)}\td{6.21 (1.24)}\td{14.58 (1.02)}}
\tr{\td{C1 only (\code{\verb~|-XX:TieredStopAtLevel=1~})}\td{1.01 (1.57)}\td{2.12 (1.38)}\td{6.26 (1.25)}\td{17.75 (1.25)}}
\tr{\td{C1 only, with the AOT cache}\td{0.93 (1.44)}\td{2.05 (1.33)}\td{6.17 (1.24)}\td{18.43 (1.30)}}
\tr{\td{compile thresholds doubled}\td{2.98 (4.60)}\td{4.34 (2.82)}\td{7.42 (1.49)}\td{15.92 (1.12)}}
\tr{\td{compile thresholds times 4}\td{2.65 (4.08)}\td{3.93 (2.55)}\td{7.19 (1.44)}\td{15.58 (1.10)}}
\tr{\td{compile thresholds times 8}\td{2.74 (4.23)}\td{3.91 (2.54)}\td{7.04 (1.41)}\td{15.79 (1.11)}}
\tr{\td{AOT cache, compile thresholds times 4}\td{1.50 (2.31)}\td{2.83 (1.83)}\td{6.08 (1.22)}\td{14.40 (1.01)}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The AOT cache of JDK 25} (\code{\verb~|-XX:AOTCacheOutput~} in a training run, \code{\verb~|-XX:AOTCache~} afterwards; JEP 483, 514, 515): recorded once per JDK with the launcher's own flags on one pass of Init, 8.2 s including the assembly by a child VM, \strong{18,677,760 bytes}; on four passes 19.3 s and 20,054,016 bytes. It holds 1,459 classes loaded and linked (259 of RLVM) and the profiles of 2,709 methods. It halves the cold run on Init (1.64 against 3.24 s) and takes 1.2 to 1.5 s off cslib and Mathlib. The cache of four passes is no better than the cache of one (Mathlib 15.27 against 14.19 s in the first window).}
\li{\strong{Less compilation helps a short run, more compilation never}: C1 alone is the fastest JVM on Init and cedar and the slowest on Mathlib (17.75 s); compile thresholds times 4 or 8 take 0.6 to 0.9 s off Init and cedar and nothing off Mathlib; thresholds halved or quartered cost 0.1 to 2.4 s everywhere. With the AOT cache as well, thresholds times 4 are level with the cache alone on Mathlib (14.40 against 14.58 s).}
\li{\strong{Compiler threads}: 4 instead of the launcher's 8 cost 0.1 to 2.7 s; 12 are level with 8.}
\li{\strong{Class-data sharing}: a dynamic archive of RLVM's own classes (4,194,304 bytes) gains nothing (Init 3.09 against 3.24 s, Mathlib 15.59 against 15.45 s), and switching the JDK's default archive off costs nothing that three rounds resolve: class loading is not where a cold JVM spends its time. The launcher's flag \code{\verb~|--enable-native-access~} makes the VM log that it cannot use the archived module graph with such an archive.}
\li{\strong{Heap}: pre-touch is off in the launcher's line and stays off (switched on: Init 3.48 against 3.24 s); a heap committed at the start gains nothing; without the huge-page advice the cold run is 0.1 to 1.8 s slower.}
\li{\strong{Not tried}: switching bytecode verification off, compact object headers, another collector, a JDK newer than 25, and anything that needs code compiled ahead of time (JDK 25 has none in its cache).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.4 The crossover for a corpus: above Mathlib, by extrapolation}

\p{\strong{No corpus gains from the hand-over in a single cold run}, with the launcher's flags or with anything of section 2.3. For an export that keeps eight workers busy the crossover lies above Mathlib, the largest export there is (6.33 GB, 721,845 units); section 2.7 has the other kind of input. What follows is an extrapolation from how the difference moves with the size, not a measurement.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The model}: a cold JVM run costs a warm-up and then a share of the executable's time, \code{\verb~|T_jvm = W + r * T_image~}. Over the three largest corpora (navier-stokes-euler, cslib, Mathlib) the least-squares line through the cells of 2.1 is \strong{W = 3.4 s, r = 0.886} for the launcher's flags, and W = 1.9 s, r = 0.894 with the AOT cache. The slope agrees with the steady passes of the two platforms on these three corpora, which stand at 0.84 to 0.87 in the benchmark rows of a quiet host ([the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks)).}
\li{\strong{Where the lines cross}: at 30 s of the executable's time with the launcher's flags, 2.1 times Mathlib, and at 18 s with the AOT cache, 1.2 times Mathlib. If time grows with the file as it does from cslib to Mathlib, that is an export of about 13 GB, and of about 8 GB with the cache.}
\li{\strong{What the gain would be there.} Past the crossover a JVM gains 0.11 s per second of the executable's time: an export twice the crossover size would be checked about 6 \% sooner.}
\li{\strong{Why the extrapolation is weak.} Three points; the warm-up itself grows with the file (the difference between a JVM's first and third pass is 2.6 s on Init and 4.0 s on Mathlib, section 2.5); and at twice Mathlib neither platform fits its present memory settings: the JVM's heap of 7 GB is the smallest at which the machine decides every unit of Mathlib ([the JVM line](rep-J1)), and the arena's runner has 16 GB.}
}
}

