\tr{\td{cslib}\td{\strong{0.913}}\td{0.87 0.94 0.94 0.91 0.91}\td{0.865}\td{1.123}\td{\strong{0.966}}\td{0.98 0.96 0.95 0.96 0.97}\td{0.982}\td{0.959}\td{\strong{1.006}}\td{1.01 1.02 1.00 1.01 1.00}\td{1.003}\td{1.003}}
\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{\strong{0.840}}\td{0.85 0.86 0.82 0.83 0.84}\td{0.824}\td{0.912}\td{\strong{1.027}}\td{1.05 1.02 1.01 1.00 1.06}\td{1.037}\td{1.027}\td{\strong{1.001}}\td{1.00 0.99 1.00 0.99 1.01}\td{1.001}\td{1.006}}
\tr{\td{\strong{geometric mean}}\td{\strong{1.010} (0.840 to 1.525)}\td{}\td{0.906}\td{1.475}\td{\strong{0.972} (0.754 to 1.051)}\td{}\td{0.988}\td{0.977}\td{\strong{1.002} (0.968 to 1.037)}\td{}\td{0.996}\td{1.005}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Three passes are not the steady state of a JVM.} Its second pass is still warm-up: 1.25 s on Init, where its third is 0.74 s and every pass of the executable 0.65 s; on a quiet host the same build's steady pass is 0.53 s on the JVM and 0.58 s in the executable ([the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks)). Taken together, passes 2 and 3 of a JVM are 1.01 times the executable's in the geometric mean: slower on reachability, Init, Std and cedar (1.02 to 1.53), faster on con-leche, navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib (0.93 to 0.84).}
\li{\strong{By the third pass the JVM leads on seven corpora}: 0.81 to 0.97 times the executable's pass, 0.82 on Mathlib (11.56 against 14.03 s); on Init its third pass is still 1.13 times.}
\li{\strong{As a whole process of three passes the JVM is ahead on Mathlib only}: 39.3 against 43.1 s, 0.91. It needs 1.12 times the executable's wall clock on cslib, 1.14 on navier-stokes-euler, 1.5 to 1.9 on the corpora of a second or two, and 2.67 on Init.}
\li{\strong{The same crossover as for one run.} The line through cslib and Mathlib is \code{\verb~|T_jvm = 5.1 s + 0.79 * T_image~} for a process of three passes: the two meet where the executable needs about 25 s for the whole process, against about 30 s for a single run (section 2.4). What decides is the time the executable would need for the process, the passes times the file, and not the number of passes.}
\li{\strong{The hybrid kept in the image is the executable}: 1.002 in the geometric mean of passes 2 and 3 (0.968 to 1.037), 1.005 for the whole process. \strong{The hybrid under auto is the JVM launcher}: 0.972 for passes 2 and 3 (0.754 to 1.051), 0.988 for pass 3, 0.977 for the whole process; section 6.3 has Std and Mathlib, the two furthest above 1, measured again.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.6 Small inputs, and what the hand-over itself costs}

\p{Whole processes again, one window of 77 s (23:42 to 23:43 UTC), the variants interleaved round by round: a tutorial test of 367 bytes, the prelude, and the largest of the arena's small tests. Mean wall clock in milliseconds with the smallest and the largest round, mean CPU time, and the largest peak resident set. The last row is the start of the executable alone.}

