\tr{\th{process}\th{tutorial test, 367 bytes, 40 rounds: wall clock, ms}\th{CPU, ms}\th{peak RSS, MiB}\th{prelude, 3.8 MB, 15 rounds: wall clock, ms}\th{CPU, ms}\th{peak RSS, MiB}\th{\code{\verb~|perf/grind-ring-5~}, 10.4 MB, 10 rounds: wall clock, ms}\th{CPU, ms}\th{peak RSS, MiB}}
\tr{\td{published executable}\td{4.52 (3.90 - 5.04)}\td{4.4}\td{21.1}\td{22.42 (20.41 - 24.82)}\td{45.7}\td{49.8}\td{165.57 (158.31 - 171.13)}\td{242.1}\td{149.2}}
\tr{\td{pristine twin}\td{4.58 (4.06 - 5.06)}\td{4.4}\td{21.2}\td{23.07 (20.81 - 25.62)}\td{46.1}\td{48.4}\td{164.64 (158.37 - 169.44)}\td{241.7}\td{150.2}}
\tr{\td{hybrid, auto}\td{4.57 (3.99 - 5.02)}\td{4.4}\td{22.0}\td{22.08 (20.97 - 24.38)}\td{45.3}\td{49.7}\td{165.73 (160.70 - 170.95)}\td{242.9}\td{149.9}}
\tr{\td{hybrid, \code{\verb~|--platform image~}}\td{4.49 (3.92 - 4.99)}\td{4.3}\td{21.9}\td{22.99 (21.00 - 25.82)}\td{45.9}\td{50.2}\td{163.70 (153.62 - 175.45)}\td{240.6}\td{151.9}}
\tr{\td{hybrid forced to the JVM, no JDK (falls back)}\td{4.89 (4.30 - 5.49)}\td{4.6}\td{22.2}\td{22.52 (21.27 - 25.01)}\td{45.6}\td{50.4}\td{166.22 (158.77 - 176.06)}\td{242.9}\td{151.0}}
\tr{\td{hybrid forced to the JVM, cache there}\td{93.25 (86.31 - 103.69)}\td{154.0}\td{81.2}\td{242.85 (229.76 - 256.34)}\td{1103.1}\td{204.3}\td{620.49 (596.19 - 643.81)}\td{3660.7}\td{387.1}}
\tr{\td{hybrid forced to the JVM, a new empty cache each time}\td{150.02 (144.12 - 157.51)}\td{251.9}\td{81.6}\td{294.99 (283.06 - 308.21)}\td{1182.9}\td{196.6}\td{681.41 (594.42 - 717.76)}\td{3776.7}\td{381.0}}
\tr{\td{JVM launcher (shell script)}\td{96.19 (88.58 - 109.27)}\td{161.7}\td{81.6}\td{242.77 (232.45 - 256.74)}\td{1084.6}\td{202.3}\td{632.21 (565.27 - 709.43)}\td{3732.1}\td{402.0}}
\tr{\td{\code{java} started directly with the hand-over's line}\td{90.07 (85.67 - 95.75)}\td{151.0}\td{81.3}\td{231.27 (214.70 - 257.83)}\td{1054.4}\td{197.4}\td{614.32 (578.81 - 644.20)}\td{3655.2}\td{385.7}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm --version~} of the hybrid}\td{3.64 (3.28 - 3.95)}\td{3.3}\td{18.4}\td{3.69 (3.54 - 4.03)}\td{3.4}\td{18.4}\td{3.54 (3.36 - 3.86)}\td{3.2}\td{18.4}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A small input is 20 to 4 times faster in the executable}: 4.5 against 93 ms on a tutorial test, 22 against 243 ms on the prelude, 166 against 620 ms on the 10 MB test, with a quarter to two fifths of the memory. The arena has 262 tests that are not corpora: 259 of them are below 8 MiB, and their median size is 6.5 kB.}
\li{\strong{The hand-over costs 3 ms}: the hybrid forced to the JVM needs 93.25 ms where \code{java} started directly with the same line needs 90.07 ms. The JVM launcher's shell script costs 6 ms on top of the same \code{java}. The 3 ms are the start of the executable (3.6 ms for \code{\verb~|--version~}, printing included) and what follows.}
\li{\strong{The steps inside the executable}, as the JVM side reports them with \code{\verb~|--stats~}, medians of 30 runs in the same window: \strong{finding the JDK 48 microseconds} (the environment, one \code{release} file, three \code{stat} calls); \strong{the cache 772 microseconds} when it is there (the directories above it checked, 13 jars read and compared, 1,108,101 bytes); \code{execv} is one system call.}
\li{\strong{The first run of a build on a machine}: unpacking the 13 jars into a new directory and reading them back takes \strong{1.6 ms}; the probe, one start of a JVM for \code{\verb~|--version~}, \strong{55 ms}. Together the first hand-over costs 57 ms more than every later one (150 against 93 ms for the whole process). A private temporary directory per run would pay the 1.6 ms every time and could not keep the probe's result, so it would either pay the 55 ms every time or go without the probe.}
\li{\strong{No JDK}: a forced hand-over that finds none costs 0.4 ms (4.89 against 4.52 ms) and then checks in-process.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.7 The inputs that do gain: a long check on one worker}

\p{The suites of section 5.3 run each of the arena's 270 tests once through the published executable and once through the hybrid forced to the JVM, in the arena-sized container. On 258 tests the JVM is slower. On twelve it is faster, and they are exactly the twelve small tests whose check takes more than a second in the executable: \code{\verb~|perf/magma-list-deep-n36~} 8.82 against 7.12 s, \code{\verb~|perf/ite-taken-branch-bad~} 5.36 against 4.22 s, \code{\verb~|perf/unfold-before-literal~} 2.13 against 1.54 s, the two tests that are declined after a long search 1.81 against 0.95 s; the next one down, \code{\verb~|perf/magma-string-pair-n9~} with 0.85 s, is 1.53 times slower on the JVM. Six of them were then timed like the corpora, the whole cold process, five interleaved rounds in one window, the arena's 8 threads named:}

\table{
