\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|perf/magma-string-pair-n9~}}\td{17,463}\td{0}\td{5 of 5}\td{0.816 (0.810 - 0.825)}\td{1.20}\td{1.30 (1.25 - 1.34)}\td{\strong{1.589}}\td{1.54 1.64 1.61 1.61 1.55}\td{1.31 (1.27 - 1.37)}\td{\strong{1.609}}\td{0.973 (0.941 - 1.029)}\td{\strong{1.192}}\td{1.11 (1.09 - 1.15)}\td{\strong{1.363}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|perf/gf3-matrix-product~}}\td{801}\td{0}\td{5 of 5}\td{1.54 (1.49 - 1.57)}\td{1.00}\td{0.998 (0.942 - 1.051)}\td{\strong{0.650}}\td{0.60 0.65 0.67 0.68 0.64}\td{0.989 (0.946 - 1.034)}\td{\strong{0.644}}\td{0.936 (0.910 - 0.974)}\td{\strong{0.610}}\td{1.82 (1.77 - 1.85)}\td{\strong{1.184}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|perf/unfold-before-literal~}}\td{67}\td{0}\td{5 of 5}\td{2.04 (2.01 - 2.09)}\td{1.00}\td{1.58 (1.55 - 1.61)}\td{\strong{0.772}}\td{0.76 0.76 0.75 0.79 0.80}\td{1.55 (1.52 - 1.57)}\td{\strong{0.759}}\td{1.35 (1.33 - 1.37)}\td{\strong{0.660}}\td{2.48 (2.42 - 2.54)}\td{\strong{1.214}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|perf/magma-list-pair-n21~}}\td{610}\td{0}\td{5 of 5}\td{2.80 (2.69 - 2.90)}\td{1.00}\td{2.03 (1.93 - 2.16)}\td{\strong{0.724}}\td{0.76 0.71 0.72 0.72 0.71}\td{2.04 (1.91 - 2.18)}\td{\strong{0.727}}\td{1.94 (1.83 - 2.10)}\td{\strong{0.692}}\td{3.21 (3.02 - 3.37)}\td{\strong{1.144}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|perf/ite-taken-branch-bad~}}\td{4,552}\td{1}\td{5 of 5}\td{5.33 (5.12 - 5.48)}\td{1.01}\td{4.30 (4.00 - 4.52)}\td{\strong{0.806}}\td{0.83 0.73 0.83 0.84 0.81}\td{4.33 (4.13 - 4.50)}\td{\strong{0.813}}\td{4.22 (4.05 - 4.35)}\td{\strong{0.792}}\td{6.02 (5.86 - 6.12)}\td{\strong{1.129}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|perf/magma-list-deep-n36~}}\td{1,279}\td{0}\td{5 of 5}\td{8.10 (7.81 - 8.35)}\td{1.00}\td{6.39 (6.23 - 6.62)}\td{\strong{0.789}}\td{0.79 0.79 0.80 0.81 0.76}\td{6.48 (6.19 - 7.08)}\td{\strong{0.800}}\td{6.68 (6.41 - 7.17)}\td{\strong{0.825}}\td{8.62 (8.12 - 9.35)}\td{\strong{1.064}}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{From a second and a half of work on one worker the JVM wins the single run}: 0.65 to 0.81 times the executable's wall clock on the five tests of 1.5 to 8 s (all 25 round ratios between 0.60 and 0.84), and 1.59 times on the test of 0.8 s. The crossover lies between 0.8 and 1.5 s of the executable's time. The hybrid forced to the JVM is the JVM launcher (0.64 to 0.81, and 1.61). The window ran on a host that had become calm (466 s, 00:48 to 00:56 UTC, no compaction stall).}
\li{\strong{Why here and not on the corpora.} These exports have few declarations and the driver gives them one worker, so the executable uses one core of eight (CPU over wall clock 1.0) and the JVM's compiler threads work on the other seven: the warm-up that costs a corpus three seconds beside eight busy workers costs little here. What is left is the code: HotSpot's C2 runs the machine and the reference engine faster than the image's compiler does, the same lead as in the warm pass. C1 alone is slower than the executable on all six (1.06 to 1.36 times), so the gain is C2's.}
\li{\strong{The AOT cache adds little}: 0.61 to 0.83 times, ahead of the plain launcher on the four shorter tests and behind on the longest.}
\li{\strong{Nothing the launcher can see tells these inputs apart.} The five that gain are files of 67 kB to 4.6 MB; the arena has small tests of 17 MB that take 0.1 s and of 4.3 MB that take 0.03 s. The time of the check is known when it is over. Starting in the executable and moving to a JVM after a second would repeat that second, and gain less than a tenth on the longest of these tests and nothing on the others; it was not built. Whoever knows that an export is of this kind can say \code{\verb~|--platform jvm~}.}
\li{\strong{In the arena's metric these tests do not gain}: the JVM executes 1.03 to 2.5 times the instructions on ten of the twelve, because the compilers count, and fewer only on the two that are declined after a long search (0.45 times).}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The design of the hybrid entry}

\p{One executable, \code{rlvm}, as today. It checks an export itself, or it replaces its own process by \code{java} on the jars it was built from. Nothing is downloaded, at build time or at run time, and without a JDK the executable is what it was.}

\subtree{
\title{3.1 One file: the jars are inside the executable}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Which jars.} The RLVM modules without \code{rlvm-truffle}, and no third-party jar: 13 files, 1,108,101 bytes. A default run loads no class of Truffle, polyglot or the SDK ([the stock-JDK report](rep-OPT-J), section 2: 244 classes, all RLVM's), and on a stock JDK the Truffle tier has only its fallback runtime; a run that names the tier (a setting \code{\verb~|engine.*~} or the machine's switch \code{t2}) stays in the executable, also when the JVM is forced. With the Truffle jars the executable would carry 20 MB instead of 1.1 MB.}
\li{\strong{How they get in.} \code{\verb~|scripts/build.sh~} copies them, with an index (the build id, the default engine, the VM flags, the heap lines, the sha256 of every jar), into a directory that it puts on the class path of the image build. The class \code{\verb~|rlvm.cli.Handover~} is initialised at image build time like the rest of the launcher's package and reads them there, checking every jar against the sha256 of the index: the bytes and the digests become data of the image, with no resource configuration. On a JVM the index is not on the class path, so jars on a JVM hand nothing over, and the class that calls \code{execv} is never loaded there.}
\li{\strong{One build id.} The JVM runs \code{\verb~|rlvm.cli.Main~} of exactly the jars the image was compiled from, with the arguments and the environment of the executable. The class path keeps the sorted order of the JVM launcher, and with it the order of the service providers.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.2 Where the jars are unpacked: a per-user cache, never trusted}

\p{The choice was between a cache directory per user, named after the build id, and a private temporary directory per run. \strong{The cache directory is the design}; a temporary directory per run was measured and not taken:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{\code{execv} leaves nobody behind to remove a temporary directory.} The process that unpacked is the JVM afterwards. A directory per run would stay until the JVM side removed it itself, which it cannot do when it is killed or runs out of memory.}
\li{\strong{The check of the JDK needs a place to remember its result} (section 3.4), and so would any class-data archive of the JDK.}
\li{\strong{The cost}: writing the 13 jars into a new directory and reading them back takes 1.6 ms, comparing the jars of a cache that is there 0.8 ms (section 2.6); so the cache saves less than a millisecond per run. What it saves is the probe: 55 ms that a directory per run would pay on every hand-over, or go without.}
}

\p{\strong{The directory} is \code{\verb~|$RLVM_CACHE_DIR/<build id>~}, else \code{\verb~|$XDG_CACHE_HOME/rlvm/<build id>~}, else \code{\verb~|$HOME/.cache/rlvm/<build id>~}. It is created with mode 0700.}

\p{\strong{The security argument.} The JVM opens the jars by path after the executable has looked at them. Two things must hold: what the JVM reads is what the executable carries, and nobody else can change that between the look and the read.}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Nothing found in the directory is trusted.} On every start every jar is read and compared with the executable's own copy, byte for byte: that is the check against the embedded digest and more, since the executable has the whole content. A file that is missing, shorter, longer, different in one byte, a symbolic link to a file with the right content, or anything that is not a regular file is not used: the executable's bytes are written to a new file beside it and renamed over it, and the result is read back. Whoever wrote into the directory before - an older run, another process of the same user, an attacker who had access once - changes nothing in what is executed.}
\li{\strong{Nobody else can change a path in between.} The directory must belong to the account that runs the process and must not be writable by its group or by others. Every directory above it, up to the root and with symbolic links resolved first, must belong to that account or to root and must not be writable by group or others, with two exceptions: a sticky directory (in \code{\verb~|/tmp~} nobody else can rename or replace an entry that belongs to this account), and a directory that nobody else can reach, because a directory further up belongs to that account or to root and is closed to group and others. If any of this fails, the run is not handed over.}
\li{\strong{What remains} is a process of the same user, or root, that replaces a jar between the comparison and the JVM's read. Such a process can replace the executable itself, or set \code{RLVM_JAVA}; the cache gives it nothing it did not have. Access control lists are covered as far as mode bits show them: the group bits of a directory with a list are its mask.}
\li{\strong{Atomic writes.} A jar appears under its name only by \code{rename}, complete. Two processes that start together on an empty cache each write their own temporary files and rename them; both leave the same bytes.}
\li{\strong{When the directory cannot be used} - \code{HOME} not set, a read-only file system, a directory above it that others can write - the run is checked by the executable. A read-only cache whose jars are intact is used.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.3 Finding a JDK without starting it}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Where}: \code{RLVM_JAVA}, a path to a java binary, and then nothing else: it is a choice made for RLVM. Otherwise \code{\verb~|$JAVA_HOME/bin/java~}, then the first \code{java} on \code{PATH}; the first usable of the two.}
\li{\strong{Usable} is decided from files, with no VM start: the binary, with symbolic links resolved, is an executable regular file; the \code{release} file of its home has a \code{JAVA_VERSION} of 25 or more and names the modules \code{\verb~|java.base~} and \code{\verb~|java.management~}, the two the jars need; and the home has HotSpot's server VM (\code{\verb~|lib/server/libjvm.so~}), because the flag line is HotSpot's. A JDK without a \code{release} file is not used.}
\li{\strong{Not found, or too old}: the executable checks, silently. \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~} has a line \code{platform:} that names the JDK it would use or the reason there is none, and \code{\verb~|--stats~} ends with \code{\verb~|platform=image|jvm~} and \code{\verb~|why=...~}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.4 The step: one probe per JDK, then \code{execv}}

\p{The executable hands over by \code{execv}: the JVM is the same process, with the same process id, standard streams, working directory and environment. A time limit, a signal or a resource limit of the caller reaches the JVM as it reached the executable, there is no second process to wait for or to orphan, and the exit status is the JVM side's own: \code{\verb~|rlvm.cli.Main~} applies \code{RLVM_STATUS_BASE} there exactly as in the image.}

\p{The price of \code{execv} is that nothing can fall back after it. A java that passes the file checks and then does not start - a damaged installation, a flag in \code{RLVM_JAVA_OPTS} that the VM refuses, a heap that cannot be reserved - would end the run with the VM's status 1, which reads as a rejection. So the first hand-over to a JDK is preceded by \strong{one probe}: the executable starts \code{\verb~|java FLAGS -cp JARS rlvm.cli.Main --version~} as a child and hands over only if it ends with status 0 and answers with the version line. The result is remembered as an empty marker file in the cache directory, named after the java binary and its VM library (path, size, modification time), the content of the \code{release} file and the complete list of VM options. A JDK updated in place, another heap or another flag gives another name and a new probe; a failed probe is not remembered, so a JDK that is repaired is used again.}

\p{\strong{What this guarantees and what it does not.} Everything that fails before \code{execv} falls back to the executable's own engine: no JDK, an old JDK, an unusable cache, a failed probe, an \code{execv} that returns. After a successful probe, a JDK that breaks without changing the size or the time of its two files is started and fails: the status is then the VM's. The arena's run line maps that to an error, never to a rejection, as it does for an executable that cannot start.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.5 The rule and the option}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The option} is \code{\verb~|--platform image|jvm|auto~}, with the environment variable \code{RLVM_PLATFORM}. It is an option of the launcher and not a setting \code{\verb~|-X platform=~}: the \code{\verb~|-X~} settings belong to the checker, which refuses a key it does not know, and the hand-over is decided before a checker exists. One module changes, \code{rlvm-cli}; on a JVM the option is accepted and changes nothing. What a run does when nothing is said is one constant: \code{auto} in the prototype as it was measured and gated, the executable with the fifth patch (section 6.4).}
\li{\strong{\code{image}}: never handed over. \strong{\code{jvm}}: always, when a JDK is usable, except for what the JVM side cannot compute alike (the Truffle tier). \strong{\code{auto}}: the default engine's plain runs only, and of those the runs with several passes, \code{\verb~|-X passes=N~} with N above 1. A single pass is never handed over, whatever the size of the export: section 2 found no corpus that gains at any size, the small tests that do gain cannot be told from the command line (section 2.7), and the code has the size threshold as one constant, set to "never". Said plainly: under \code{auto}, the only runs that leave the executable are runs of the option with which the warm metric is measured.}
\li{\strong{What \code{auto} never hands over}: a run with a named checker, with the reference engine or the strict policy, or with a VM option addressed to the executable (\code{\verb~|-Xmx~}, \code{\verb~|-XX:~}, \code{\verb~|-D~}: the executable has consumed it before \code{main}, and \code{\verb~|/proc/self/cmdline~} shows it). A forced hand-over passes such options on to the JVM, and a heap option replaces the default heap, as the JVM launcher does.}
\li{\strong{A run that is not handed over does nothing of this.} The rule reads the command line and the size of the export. The JDK is searched and the cache touched only after the rule has said "hand over".}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.6 The VM flags and the heap}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The flags} are the JVM launcher's line (\code{JVM_FLAGS} of \src{scripts/build.sh}) with two differences. The two options that silence JDK warnings about Truffle (\code{\verb~|--enable-native-access~}, \code{\verb~|--sun-misc-unsafe-memory-access~}) are left out: nothing of Truffle is handed over, and a JDK after 25 may refuse them. \code{\verb~|-XX:+IgnoreUnrecognizedVMOptions~} is put in front, so that a later JDK that has dropped one of the flags starts.}
\li{\strong{The heap} is the JVM launcher's by engine: \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~} for the machine, \code{\verb~|-Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g~} for any other engine.}
\li{\strong{A machine with less memory} than the heap and 1 GiB beside it (physical memory, or the limit of the control group if that is smaller): \code{auto} does not hand over, because the executable's heap grows as needed and the JVM's young generation is a gigabyte from the start; a forced hand-over gets three quarters of the memory as heap and at most a quarter of that as young generation.}
\li{\strong{No AOT cache.} The cache of section 2.3 is the one thing that brings a cold JVM close to the executable, and the hybrid does not use one. It is tied to the JDK build and to the jars, so it would have to be recorded on the machine where it is used, by a training run of some export, into a file of 19 MB per JDK; the VM maps that file into itself, and nothing in the executable can say what it must contain, so the rule of section 3.2 - nothing found in the cache is trusted - cannot hold for it; it serves the first pass, and the runs that \code{auto} hands over are the ones with several passes, where [the JVM line](rep-J1) found every later pass 5 to 9 \% slower with it (on an earlier build; not measured again here); and with it no corpus gains in a single run either. \code{RLVM_JAVA_OPTS} can name one.}
\li{\code{RLVM_JAVA_OPTS} replaces flags and heap, as it does for the JVM launcher.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The prototype}

\p{Built in a private copy of the tracked sources and delivered as five patches against the main tree; nothing was applied, installed or published. The patches name this report as their note. Patches 1 to 4 are the hybrid that was measured and gated, in which \code{auto} is what a run does when nothing is said; patch 5, on top, is the verdict of section 6: the executable is the default and the hand-over an option.}

\table{
\tr{\th{patch}\th{files}\th{what}}
\tr{\td{1, \code{rlvm-cli}}\td{\code{\verb~|Handover.java~} (new), \code{\verb~|NativeExec.java~} (new), \code{\verb~|Main.java~}, \code{\verb~|HandoverTest.java~} (new, 18 tests), the module's README}\td{the rule, the search for a JDK, the cache, the probe, the command line of the JVM; \code{execv} through the C interface of the image; the option \code{\verb~|--platform~} and \code{RLVM_PLATFORM}, the hook in front of the check, \code{\verb~|platform=~} and \code{\verb~|why=~} at the end of the \code{\verb~|--stats~} line, the line \code{platform:} of \code{\verb~|--version~}, the help text}}
\tr{\td{2, the build}\td{\src{scripts/build.sh}}\td{the jars and their index onto the class path of the image build; the flag line of the hand-over; a line in the build record; and a verification of every executable it builds: forced to the JVM and with two passes a tutorial test must be accepted by a JVM, with the status base carried through; without an option, with \code{\verb~|--platform image~}, with a java that does not exist and with a cache that cannot be made it must be accepted by the executable}}
\tr{\td{3, the README}\td{\src{README.md}}\td{the option and a paragraph "With a JDK"}}
\tr{\td{4, two tools}\td{\src{bench/warm.py}, \src{bench/track/track.py}}\td{they set \code{\verb~|RLVM_PLATFORM=image~} when they time the executable with several passes (section 7)}}
\tr{\td{5, the hand-over as an option}\td{\code{\verb~|Handover.java~}, \code{\verb~|Main.java~}, \code{\verb~|HandoverTest.java~}, \src{scripts/build.sh}, the two READMEs}\td{the default becomes the executable (one constant, \code{\verb~|Handover.Platform.DEFAULT~}); the line of \code{\verb~|--version~} and the help text say so; the unit test and the build's verification expect it}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Only \code{rlvm-cli} changes among the jars}: the hybrid distribution was built from the published jars with \code{rlvm-cli} and \code{rlvm-main} compiled from the tree, and 13 of its 14 RLVM jars have the sha256 of the published build's; so has every jar of the pristine twin, which was built the same way from the unchanged sources as the control of this work (build id \code{f5517aa135d2}, the published one). The hybrid's build id is \code{f8d3bfd23fcc}.}
\li{\strong{The executable} is 69,994,616 bytes against 68,421,752: 1,108,101 bytes of jars and 0.46 MB of code the launcher did not need before (starting a process, file attributes). Both images were built in 101 s side by side.}
\li{\strong{\code{execv} in a native image}: one method annotated as a C function, in a class that nothing loads on a JVM. The image builder takes a C function without a context as one of the C library, with no header and no extra library; the executable still links only the C library and zlib. The argument vector is copied to memory outside the heap; if \code{execv} returns, the memory is freed and the check goes on in-process.}
\li{\strong{What the JVM inherits}, compared on a live process with the JVM launcher's JVM: the same executable (\code{java}), the same signal state (nothing blocked, nothing ignored, the same handlers), the standard streams. \strong{One descriptor more}: the image runtime opens its own executable file without close-on-exec, and the JVM keeps that read-only descriptor to the end. It is harmless and it is not tidy; the remedy, \code{close_range} with \code{CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC} before \code{execv}, was not put in, because the sources were frozen for the gates by then.}
\li{\strong{\code{--version}} of the hybrid executable has one line more, here on a machine whose only java is a JDK 8 without a release file (the home directory shortened):}
}

\pre{\verb~|
platform: auto - the executable checks; a JVM (JDK 25 or newer: RLVM_JAVA, JAVA_HOME, java on PATH) is handed -X passes=N with N above 1 (default engine, no VM option of the executable); --platform image|jvm|auto. JDK: none usable [PATH:no-release-file]: the executable checks everything itself; jars: 13 in the executable (1108101 bytes, build f8d3bfd23fcc), unpacked to $HOME/.cache/rlvm/f8d3bfd23fcc
~}

\p{and the end of a \code{\verb~|--stats~} line, of a run that stayed and of one that was handed over (JDK and cache paths shortened):}

\pre{\verb~|
... heap_max_kb=13631488 os_threads=3 platform=image why=auto:single-pass
... heap_max_kb=7235200 os_threads=19 platform=jvm why=handed-over(rule=forced,java=JDK/bin/java,jdk=25.0.5.1,from=JAVA_HOME,jars=CACHE/f8d3bfd23fcc,find_us=86,cache_us=1181,probe_us=0)
~}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Gates, all on the hybrid distribution}

\subtree{
\title{5.1 The suites and the gate of the default configuration in each mode}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The suite of \code{rlvm-cli}}, the one module that changed: 67 tests, 0 failures, 0 skipped (49 before; the 18 new ones are the rule, the search for a JDK with directories that look like JDKs of 8, 21, 25, 26 and 27, the cache with damaged, linked and foreign files and open directories above it, the heap rule and the command line).}
\li{\strong{\code{scripts/gate.sh --defaults --corpus all --hostile --native}} through the hybrid executable, once per mode, the mode given as \code{RLVM_PLATFORM} in the gate's environment, and once through the jars. A JDK 25 was there in every mode, as \code{RLVM_JAVA}: a directory that looks like the Temurin JDK whose \code{java} appends a line to a file and then becomes Temurin's, so the JVM starts of a gate are counted. All on the efficiency cores beside the windows.}
}

\table{
\tr{\th{gate}\th{230 published tests}\th{17,775 mutants: SOUNDNESS / COMPLETENESS, agree, declined, error}\th{hostile exports, 1,274 rows: agree, accepts of kind T, wrong accept / wrong reject, declined}\th{JVM starts}\th{result}}
\tr{\td{executable, \code{image}}\td{230 correct, 0 declined}\td{0 / 0, 15,739, 2,034, 2}\td{1,192, 78, 0 / 0, 4}\td{1 (the differential self-test, which runs the JVM launcher); no jar unpacked}\td{PASS}}
\tr{\td{executable, \code{auto}}\td{230 correct, 0 declined}\td{0 / 0, 15,739, 2,034, 2}\td{1,192, 78, 0 / 0, 4}\td{1 (the same); no jar unpacked}\td{PASS}}
\tr{\td{executable, \code{jvm}}\td{230 correct, 0 declined}\td{0 / 0, 15,741, 2,034, 0}\td{1,192, 78, 0 / 0, 4}\td{\strong{19,288}: the 19,279 checked files, 8 probes, 1 run of the differential self-test}\td{PASS}}
\tr{\td{the jars (JVM launcher)}\td{230 correct, 0 declined}\td{0 / 0, 15,741, 2,034, 0}\td{1,192, 78, 0 / 0, 4}\td{-}\td{PASS}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{No wrong accept and no wrong reject in any mode.} The two mutant runs that end with an error are the two \code{CHANGE_LITERAL} mutants of the corpus \code{bugs} that reach the gate's limit of 60 s when the host is loaded; they are known from the gates of the published build under load. Each alone and without the limit, both are rejected, as the official checker rejects them, by the published executable and by the hybrid in each of its three modes: 14 to 18 s in the executable and 9 and 12 s on the JVM, on two cores.}
\li{\strong{Under \code{auto} the gate is the image's gate}: a single pass is never handed over, so no JVM was started for a checked file and nothing was unpacked. That is the mode a user has without saying anything.}
\li{\strong{Forced to the JVM, every one of the 19,279 files of the gate was checked by a JVM}, 51 minutes on the efficiency cores of a loaded host: the wrapper counted 19,288 starts, which are the 230 tests, the 17,775 mutants and the 1,274 hostile rows, one start of the JVM launcher by the differential self-test, and eight probes - the gate's four lanes started together on an empty cache, and each probed the JDK once for each of the gate's two command lines (the run line, and the mutants' line with its heap cap of 4 GB, which the hand-over passes on to the JVM). With a JVM under them the two heavy mutants stay inside their limit, so this gate has no error at all and the agreement count is the full 15,741.}
}
}

