}

\ul{
\li{\strong{6,888 values in 32 comparisons, none different.} The hybrid executable computes what the published one does (the jars of the engines are the same files), under \code{auto} what it computes under \code{image}, and after a hand-over what it computes itself: 559,746,444 ticks of the machine on Mathlib through all five, the same records, run words, pairs, the same 128 units declined by the machine and decided by the reference engine. All 40 runs accept with status 0 and print no line that is not the checker's.}
\li{\strong{The eight corpora with 8 and 4 workers}, a run without \code{\verb~|-X~}: through the hybrid executable with \code{\verb~|--platform image~}, without an option and with \code{\verb~|--platform jvm~}, and through the JVM launcher of the hybrid distribution: \strong{64 of 64 runs accept with status 0}; the statistics line says \code{\verb~|platform=image why=forced~}, \code{\verb~|why=auto:single-pass~}, \code{\verb~|platform=jvm why=handed-over(rule=forced,...)~} and \code{\verb~|why=started-on-a-jvm~} in the 16 runs of each.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.3 The arena's own procedure, with a JDK and with none}

\p{The procedure of [AR2](rep-AR2): the run line of \src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml}, verbatim, over the arena's complete test set (148 good with the eight corpora, 92 bad, 30 either) in one container of the arena's size (8 performance cores, 16 GB, no network), one test after the other under the arena's time limits, with the instruction counter around every run line, which counts the instructions that all processes of a test execute in user mode. Six suites, each in a quiet window of its own; what differs is the environment of the checker. "A JDK" is Temurin 25 in \code{JAVA_HOME} and first on \code{PATH}, as a stock JDK is in the arena's shell; "no JDK" is an empty \code{JAVA_HOME} and no java on \code{PATH}. The cache is a directory of the container's own \code{\verb~|/tmp~}, empty at the start of every suite.}

\table{
\tr{\th{suite}\th{JDK in the checker's environment}\th{good: accepted / rejected / declined}\th{bad: rejected / accepted / declined}\th{either: accepted / rejected / declined}\th{error, timeout}\th{wrong accepts / wrong rejects}\th{same status and exit code as the published executable}\th{peak RSS of the 262 small tests, MiB: smallest - median}\th{largest share of a time limit}\th{suite, s}}
\tr{\td{the published executable}\td{as the shell of the container has it}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{11 / 17 / 2}\td{0}\td{0 / 0}\td{270 of 270}\td{18.1 - 21.7}\td{5 \% (5.4 s of 110 s)}\td{88}}
\tr{\td{hybrid, nothing said (auto)}\td{a JDK 25}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{11 / 17 / 2}\td{0}\td{0 / 0}\td{270 of 270}\td{18.8 - 22.6}\td{5 \% (5.3 s of 110 s)}\td{88}}
\tr{\td{hybrid, \code{\verb~|RLVM_PLATFORM=image~}}\td{a JDK 25}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{11 / 17 / 2}\td{0}\td{0 / 0}\td{270 of 270}\td{19.0 - 22.6}\td{5 \% (5.5 s of 110 s)}\td{89}}
\tr{\td{hybrid, \code{\verb~|RLVM_PLATFORM=jvm~}}\td{a JDK 25}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{11 / 17 / 2}\td{0}\td{0 / 0}\td{270 of 270}\td{68.7 - 79.3}\td{4 \% (4.2 s of 110 s)}\td{127}}
\tr{\td{hybrid, nothing said (auto)}\td{no JDK}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{11 / 17 / 2}\td{0}\td{0 / 0}\td{270 of 270}\td{18.9 - 22.6}\td{5 \% (5.2 s of 110 s)}\td{86}}
\tr{\td{hybrid, \code{\verb~|RLVM_PLATFORM=jvm~}}\td{no JDK}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{11 / 17 / 2}\td{0}\td{0 / 0}\td{270 of 270}\td{19.0 - 22.7}\td{5 \% (5.4 s of 110 s)}\td{86}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The same status and the same exit code on 270 of 270 tests in all six suites}, and the same output: the text a test wrote to standard output and standard error, as the suite keeps it, is identical between the published executable and each of the five hybrid suites on all 270. The table is the one of [AR2](rep-AR2): 148 good accepted, 92 bad rejected, of the 30 either 11 accepted, 17 rejected and the two on which the official kernel does not terminate declined. No wrong accept, no wrong reject, no error, no timeout.}
\li{\strong{With a JDK and nothing said, nothing is handed over}: the suite under \code{auto} is the image's suite, in verdicts, in instructions (next section) and in memory.}
\li{\strong{Forced to the JVM with a JDK, every test ran on a JVM}: the smallest resident set of the 270 is 68.7 MiB and the fewest instructions 554 M, where the executable needs 18 to 22 MiB and 4 M for a small test. The suite takes 127 s instead of 88 s, and the declines stay declines: the two tests that are declined after 1.9 s of work are declined by the JVM too, with exit status 2 through the run line.}
\li{\strong{Forced to the JVM without a JDK, every test falls back} and the suite is the image's again: 270 of 270 with the same status, exit code and output, the instructions within 0.2 \% of the published executable's on every corpus and 1.001 times on the sum of the small tests, a median resident set of 22.7 MiB for a small test. In the container the hybrid says \code{\verb~|JDK: none usable [no-jdk(RLVM_JAVA-unset,JAVA_HOME-unset,no-java-on-PATH)]~} with \code{\verb~|--version~} and \code{\verb~|why=fallback(forced):no-jdk(...)~} with \code{\verb~|--stats~}.}
\li{\strong{The cache needed a place in the container}: its home directory lies inside the checkout, below directories that the group can write, and with the default place a forced run there says \code{\verb~|why=fallback(forced):cache:directory-above-the-cache-is-not-safe:...~} and is checked by the executable. The suites gave the cache a directory of the container's \code{\verb~|/tmp~}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.4 What the hand-over does to the arena's metric}

\p{The arena ranks by the instructions of a Mathlib run and counts the instructions of every process of a test. From the same six suites: user-mode instructions, wall clock and peak resident set of the published executable and of the hybrid forced to the JVM, and the instructions of the hybrid under \code{auto} and \code{image} as a ratio to the published executable's.}

\table{
\tr{\th{test}\th{published executable: instructions, G}\th{wall, s}\th{hybrid, auto: instructions / published}\th{hybrid, image: instructions / published}\th{hybrid forced to the JVM: instructions, G}\th{/ published}\th{wall, s}\th{/ published}\th{peak RSS, MiB: published}\th{forced to the JVM}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{15.8}\td{0.54}\td{0.999}\td{1.000}\td{62.1}\td{\strong{3.923}}\td{1.16}\td{2.155}\td{672}\td{1,381}}
\tr{\td{init}\td{68.3}\td{0.65}\td{1.000}\td{1.000}\td{179.9}\td{\strong{2.635}}\td{3.09}\td{4.781}\td{365}\td{705}}
\tr{\td{std}\td{118.4}\td{1.23}\td{0.998}\td{0.999}\td{243.5}\td{\strong{2.057}}\td{3.94}\td{3.208}\td{569}\td{1,352}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{148.9}\td{1.47}\td{1.000}\td{0.999}\td{283.9}\td{\strong{1.907}}\td{4.51}\td{3.071}\td{845}\td{1,440}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{167.0}\td{1.60}\td{1.000}\td{1.000}\td{282.3}\td{\strong{1.690}}\td{4.30}\td{2.684}\td{737}\td{1,541}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{415.8}\td{3.96}\td{1.000}\td{1.001}\td{491.2}\td{\strong{1.181}}\td{6.11}\td{1.544}\td{1,288}\td{2,271}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{480.1}\td{5.00}\td{1.000}\td{1.000}\td{566.6}\td{\strong{1.180}}\td{7.76}\td{1.553}\td{1,552}\td{2,354}}
\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{1,432.7}\td{14.10}\td{1.000}\td{1.000}\td{1,347.3}\td{\strong{0.940}}\td{15.69}\td{1.112}\td{3,565}\td{4,741}}
\tr{\td{the 262 other tests, sum}\td{621.1}\td{51.81}\td{1.001}\td{1.001}\td{1,308.2}\td{\strong{2.106}}\td{68.95}\td{1.331}\td{2,369 (largest)}\td{3,397 (largest)}}
\tr{\td{the median of the 262}\td{0.0042}\td{0.0056}\td{1.003}\td{1.004}\td{0.7769}\td{\strong{184}}\td{0.1030}\td{18.5}\td{21.7}\td{79.3}}
\tr{\td{all 270 tests, sum}\td{3,468.1}\td{80.35}\td{1.000}\td{1.000}\td{4,765.0}\td{\strong{1.374}}\td{115.50}\td{1.438}\td{}\td{}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Under \code{auto} and \code{image} the instruction counts are the published executable's}: 0.998 to 1.001 on every corpus, 1,433.1 against 1,432.7 G on Mathlib, 1.001 on the sum of the 262 small tests. The median small test needs 4.24 M instructions against 4.23 M: about ten thousand instructions for the rule.}
\li{\strong{The hand-over lowers the Mathlib count by 6 \%}: 1,347.3 against 1,432.7 G, at 1.11 times the wall clock (15.69 against 14.10 s) and 4,741 against 3,565 MiB. Mathlib and the two tests that are declined after a long search are the only three of the 270 on which a JVM executes fewer instructions; twelve tests, all heavy ones of a second or more, have a shorter wall clock.}
\li{\strong{It multiplies the count of a small test by 184}: 777 M against 4.2 M instructions in the median, 103 against 5.6 ms, 79 against 22 MiB. Over the 262 small tests the sum doubles, from 621 to 1,308 G, and over all 270 tests it is 1.37 times the executable's: the 687 G that the small tests cost more are eight times the 85 G that Mathlib costs less.}
\li{\strong{So for the arena}: a hand-over of every test would move the ranking number down by 6 \% and every other number up; a hand-over of Mathlib alone, by size, would keep the first and avoid the second, at 11 \% more wall clock for that test. Neither is what \code{auto} does: its rule follows the wall clock of one run, which is the maintainer's metric and which no test gains. The counts are those of this host; the arena's runner is x86-64.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.5 Behaviour when the hand-over cannot happen}

\p{A script of 69 cases and one stated limit, each a run of the hybrid executable on a tutorial test in an environment that contains only what the case names; expected are the exit status and the last words of the \code{\verb~|--stats~} line. All 69 pass.}

\table{
\tr{\th{case}\th{what happens}\th{\code{why=} of the statistics line}}
\tr{\td{no JDK at all (nothing set, an empty \code{PATH}); forced, or auto with two passes}\td{checked by the executable, status 0 (1 for a bad test)}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):no-jdk(RLVM_JAVA-unset,JAVA_HOME-unset,no-java-on-PATH)~}}}
\tr{\td{the host's own \code{java}: OpenJDK 8, which has no \code{release} file}\td{the same}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):PATH:no-release-file~}}}
\tr{\td{a real JDK 21 (Temurin 21.0.13.1+2) as \code{RLVM_JAVA}, as \code{JAVA_HOME}, on \code{PATH}}\td{the same; no VM is started to find out}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):JAVA_HOME:java-21.0.13.1-is-older-than-25~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{JAVA_HOME} is the JDK 21, \code{PATH} has a JDK 25}\td{handed over to the JDK 25}\td{\code{\verb~|handed-over(rule=forced,...,from=PATH,...)~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{RLVM_JAVA} is the JDK 21, \code{PATH} has a JDK 25}\td{checked by the executable: \code{RLVM_JAVA} is the only candidate when it is set}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):RLVM_JAVA:java-21.0.13.1-is-older-than-25~}}}
\tr{\td{Temurin 25 by each of the three ways; GraalVM CE 25 as \code{JAVA_HOME}}\td{handed over; status 0 and 1, and 10 and 11 with \code{\verb~|RLVM_STATUS_BASE=10~}; an unknown \code{\verb~|-X~} setting is 64 on the JVM side too}\td{\code{\verb~|handed-over(rule=forced,...)~}}}
\tr{\td{a broken java beside a valid \code{release} file: a script that exits with 1, a binary cut off after 4 KiB, an empty file, a program that prints something else and exits with 0}\td{the probe fails, the executable checks, status 0}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):jdk-does-not-start(RLVM_JAVA)~}}}
\tr{\td{a java that does not exist; a directory}\td{checked by the executable}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):RLVM_JAVA:NoSuchFileException~}, \code{\verb~|...:not-an-executable-file~}}}
\tr{\td{a flag in \code{RLVM_JAVA_OPTS} that the VM refuses}\td{the probe fails, the executable checks}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):jdk-does-not-start(JAVA_HOME)~}}}
\tr{\td{\strong{the limit}: a program that answers the probe like these jars and then exits with 1}\td{after \code{execv} the status is that program's: 1. Through the arena's run line it is 3, an error}\td{(no statistics: the process is no longer RLVM)}}
\tr{\td{\code{HOME} not set and no \code{RLVM_CACHE_DIR}}\td{checked by the executable}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):no-cache-directory(HOME-unset)~}}}
\tr{\td{the place for the cache is read-only; a file is where the directory should be}\td{checked by the executable}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):cache:AccessDeniedException~}, \code{\verb~|cache:FileSystemException~}}}
\tr{\td{the cache is read-only and its jars are intact}\td{handed over}\td{}}
\tr{\td{the cache is read-only and one jar has a byte appended}\td{checked by the executable; once it is writable again the jar is rewritten, byte for byte the distribution's}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):cache:AccessDeniedException~}}}
\tr{\td{one byte of a jar flipped, same length; a jar replaced by a symbolic link to a file with the right bytes}\td{the jar is rewritten as a regular file, then handed over}\td{}}
\tr{\td{a directory where a jar should be}\td{checked by the executable}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):cache:FileSystemException~}}}
\tr{\td{the cache directory writable by its group}\td{checked by the executable}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):cache:cache-directory-not-private:...~}}}
\tr{\td{a directory above the cache writable by everybody: below a directory closed to others / reachable by others / reachable and sticky}\td{handed over / checked by the executable / handed over}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(forced):cache:directory-above-the-cache-is-not-safe:...~}}}
\tr{\td{a cache inside the checkout of the development host, whose directories are group-writable}\td{checked by the executable}\td{the same}}
\tr{\td{2, 8 and 16 processes started together on an empty cache, three times each, good and bad tests mixed}\td{every process handed over with the right status; 14 files in the cache afterwards (13 jars and the marker), no temporary file left}\td{}}
\tr{\td{a container limited to 4 GB of memory (by hand, outside the script)}\td{forced: handed over with a heap of 3 GB (\code{\verb~|heap_max_kb=3067136~}); auto with two passes: checked by the executable}\td{\code{\verb~|fallback(auto:passes=2):low-memory~}}}
\tr{\td{forced with \code{\verb~|-Xmx2g~}; with the reference engine or the strict policy}\td{handed over with a heap of 2 GB; of 13 GB}\td{}}
\tr{\td{auto with two passes and \code{\verb~|-Xmx2g~}, the reference engine, or \code{\verb~|--checker rlvm~}; forced with the machine's switch \code{t2}}\td{checked by the executable}\td{\code{\verb~|auto:vm-options-of-the-executable~}, \code{\verb~|auto:engine-reference~}, \code{\verb~|auto:checker-named~}, \code{\verb~|truffle-tier-is-in-the-executable-only~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|--protocol-selftest~} with \code{\verb~|RLVM_PLATFORM=jvm~}; \code{\verb~|--platform hotspot~}}\td{never handed over; a usage error, 64}\td{}}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. The guard for making \code{auto} the default, and the verdict}

\p{The guard, as the maintainer set it: (1) no corpus slower in a single cold run than the executable alone by more than the window resolves; (2) small inputs untouched: a tutorial test stays at its 0.014 s and 20 MB; (3) the warm metric with \code{\verb~|-X passes=3~} no slower than the JVM launcher.}

\subtree{
\title{6.1 One cold run under auto against the executable alone}

\p{Under \code{auto} a single pass is never handed over, so the hybrid executable does what the executable does; what could differ is the binary, which is 1.5 MB larger. Two windows. In the window of section 2.1 the published executable, the pristine twin (the same sources and jars as the published build, built again by this work beside the hybrid) and the hybrid under \code{auto} ran in every round, always in that order, five rounds:}

