}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The instruction counts settle what the wall clock cannot}: in the arena's procedure the hybrid under \code{auto} needs 0.998 to 1.001 times the published executable's instructions on the eight corpora (1,433.1 against 1,432.7 G on Mathlib) and 1.001 times on the sum of the 262 small tests: the same work (section 5.4).}
\li{\strong{The hybrid forced to the JVM against the JVM launcher} (last columns of the first table): 1.015 in the geometric mean, 0.981 to 1.099, within what five cold JVM runs resolve; Mathlib 0.988.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.2 Small inputs}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The time is untouched}: a tutorial test takes 4.57 ms through the hybrid under \code{auto} and 4.52 ms through the published executable (4.58 ms through the pristine twin; 40 interleaved rounds each, section 2.6); the prelude 22.1 against 22.4 ms; the 10 MB test 165.7 against 165.6 ms. The rule looks at the command line and makes one \code{stat} of the export.}
\li{\strong{The resident set is 0.9 MiB larger}: 22.0 MiB against 21.1 MiB for the tutorial test (21.9 with \code{\verb~|--platform image~}, 21.2 for the twin). The jars are 1.1 MiB of the image heap (26.6 against 25.5 MiB in the file), and the resident set of a bare start grows by about as much: 17.9 against 16.5 MB for \code{\verb~|--version~}. On the prelude and on the 10 MB test the difference is inside the spread of the runs (49.7 against 49.8 MiB, 149.9 against 149.2 MiB). This is a cost of having the jars inside the executable, whatever the default is; keeping them outside the image heap, appended to the file and read only at a hand-over, would remove it and was not built.}
\li{The brief names 0.014 s and 20 MB for a tutorial test. Measured here, on the cores of a quiet window, the published executable needs 4.5 ms and 21.1 MiB for it; in the arena-sized container (section 5.3) the median of the 262 small tests is 5.6 ms and 21.7 MiB through the published executable, and 5.8 ms, 22.6 MiB and 4.24 against 4.23 M instructions through the hybrid under \code{auto}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.3 Three passes under auto against the JVM launcher}

\p{Under \code{auto} a run with several passes is handed over, so the question is whether the JVM the executable hands over to is as fast as the JVM the launcher starts. The two differ in two flags that are left out, one that is added and nine jars that are not on the class path (section 3.6). In the window of section 2.5, five rounds, the hybrid under \code{auto} needs 0.972 times the launcher's passes 2 and 3 in the geometric mean of the eight corpora, 0.988 for pass 3 and 0.977 for the whole process; four corpora are between 0.966 and 0.999, Init is at 0.754 (two rounds in which the launcher's second pass was long), and three are above 1: reachability 1.022, Mathlib 1.027 and Std 1.051. Std and Mathlib were measured again, six rounds, the order of the processes drawn anew in every round, with the JVM launcher of the hybrid distribution as a third process (00:31 to 00:48 UTC, still the evening of 2026-11-25 in the journal's time zone; 1,019 s; on Mathlib two of eight rounds had compaction stalls and are left out):}

