\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{\strong{0.985}}\td{1.00 1.03 1.00 0.89 1.00 0.99}\td{0.987}\td{0.989}\td{\strong{0.985}}\td{0.98 0.98 1.00 0.97 0.98 0.99}\td{0.983}\td{0.985}\td{\strong{1.000}}\td{1.02 1.05 1.00 0.92 1.02 1.00}\td{1.004}\td{1.004}}
\tr{\td{\strong{geometric mean}}\td{\strong{0.998} (0.985 to 1.012)}\td{}\td{0.993}\td{1.013}\td{\strong{0.975} (0.965 to 0.985)}\td{}\td{0.985}\td{0.973}\td{\strong{1.024} (1.000 to 1.048)}\td{}\td{1.008}\td{1.041}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{No difference that holds}: the hybrid under \code{auto} against the JVM launcher of the published build is 1.012 on Std and \strong{0.985 on Mathlib} for passes 2 and 3, and 0.998 and 0.987 for pass 3 alone; against the launcher of its own distribution 0.965 and 0.985. What the first window showed on these two corpora did not repeat, and on Mathlib it has the other sign.}
\li{\strong{The warm pass is the JVM launcher's}: 10.4 to 11.6 s for the third pass of Mathlib on all three, where the executable needs 14.0 s in the same hour.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.4 Verdict}

\p{\strong{NO-GO for \code{auto} as the default. GO for the hybrid as an option}: \code{\verb~|--platform jvm|auto~} and \code{RLVM_PLATFORM}, with the executable as what a run does when nothing is said.}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Item 1 holds.} No corpus is slower under \code{auto} than the executable alone by more than the windows resolve (0.970 to 1.023 where the control pair is 0.992 to 1.020), and the instructions are the same.}
\li{\strong{Item 2 holds for the time and not for the memory.} A tutorial test takes the same 4.5 ms. Its resident set is 22.0 MiB where it was 21.1 MiB, and the median small test of the arena has 22.6 MiB where it had 21.7: 0.9 MiB, the jars in the image heap, measured in every run. It is small. The guard says untouched, and this report does not bend it.}
\li{\strong{Item 3 holds.} With three passes the hybrid under \code{auto} is the JVM launcher, within what the windows resolve, and on Mathlib its third pass is the launcher's 10.4 to 11.6 s.}
\li{\strong{Beyond the guard: what \code{auto} would do as the default is little, and it has a price.} For a single run it changes nothing, by its own rule, because no corpus gains; the twelve small tests that would gain cannot be recognised before the check, so \code{auto} leaves them where they are. What it moves to a JVM is a run with \code{\verb~|-X passes=N~}, the option with which the warm metric is measured: there the third pass is faster on seven corpora, passes 2 and 3 together are not yet faster on the four smallest, and the process as a whole is faster on Mathlib alone (section 2.5). In exchange, every tool that times the executable with several passes would measure a JVM unless it says \code{\verb~|RLVM_PLATFORM=image~} (section 7).}
\li{\strong{The threshold}: there is none to give. For an export that keeps eight workers busy the crossover lies at about twice Mathlib, by extrapolation; for a check on one worker it lies at 0.8 to 1.5 s of the check, which no property of the file predicts. The code keeps the size threshold as one constant, set to "never".}
\li{\strong{What is delivered for this verdict}: patches 1 to 4 are the hybrid as it was built, measured and gated, with \code{auto} as the default of the code. \strong{Patch 5 makes it an option}: the default is the executable; it changes one constant of the launcher, the wording of \code{\verb~|--version~} and \code{\verb~|--help~}, the expectations of one unit test and of the build's verification, and the two READMEs. With patch 5: the suite of \code{rlvm-cli} 67 of 67; the build's verification passes; the behaviour script passes its 69 cases under \code{\verb~|RLVM_PLATFORM=auto~} and six more on the default (nothing said, one pass and two passes, with a JDK there: checked by the executable, \code{\verb~|why=forced~}); the gate of the default configuration through the executable with nothing said and a JDK there passes (230 of 230, 17,775 mutants 0 / 0 with 15,741 agreeing, 1,274 hostile rows 0 / 0) with no JVM started for a checked file; the gate through its jars passes too (230 of 230, 0 / 0 with 15,741 agreeing, 0 / 0). Its timing windows were not repeated: it differs from the measured executable by that constant.}
\li{\strong{What would change the verdict}: the jars kept outside the image heap, which removes the 0.9 MiB; and a use for \code{auto} - an export of twice Mathlib's size, or a way to know a long single-worker check before it starts.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. What an integrator must know}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Five patches, in this order}: \code{rlvm-cli}, the build, the README, the two tools, and the fifth, which makes the hand-over an option. Patches 1 to 4 alone are the hybrid with \code{auto} as its default, as it was measured and gated; with patch 5 it is what section 6.4 recommends. Only \code{rlvm-cli} is a jar that changes; \code{rlvm-main} is untouched.}
\li{\strong{The main tree moved while this work ran}: a change of the driver, of \code{rlvm-main} and of the gate scripts went in at 22:44 UTC with a new shared build. The hybrid was built, measured and gated on the build published at 20:00 UTC, which is the one the brief names: the published jars of that build plus this work's \code{rlvm-cli}. The gates ran with the main tree's gate scripts as they were when each gate started, all after that change: their hostile manifest has 1,274 rows and marks 12 of them as known wrong rejects of the newer build, of which the hybrid on the build of 20:00 has none. None of the files the patches touch changed in the main tree except \src{README.md}; the patches were made again against the main tree at the end of the work and checked by applying them, in order, to a copy of its tracked files: the files they touch come out as they were built and gated.}
\li{\strong{\code{-X passes=N} on the executable is no longer a measurement of the executable} when a JDK is found and the default is \code{auto}. Everything that times the image with several passes must say so: \code{\verb~|RLVM_PLATFORM=image~} in its environment, which an executable from before this change ignores. Patch 4 does it for \src{bench/warm.py} and \src{bench/track/track.py}. The private scripts of the optimisation waves that run \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm -X passes=N~} are not in the repository and are not patched: with \code{JAVA_HOME} set by \src{env.sh} they would time a JVM and call it the image. The guard of [the default switch set](dec-YP0N) is measured that way.}
\li{\strong{The default is one constant}: with patch 5 it is \code{\verb~|Handover.Platform.DEFAULT~}, set to \code{IMAGE}; set to \code{AUTO} it gives the behaviour of patches 1 to 4, and then the two expectations of the build's verification and of the unit test that patch 5 changed change back. With the executable as the default, the item before this one arises only for a tool that itself asks for \code{auto}. The rule's size threshold is the constant \code{\verb~|Handover.AUTO_MIN_BYTES~}, set to "never".}
\li{\strong{\code{RLVM_JAVA} and \code{RLVM_JAVA_OPTS} mean for the executable what they mean for the JVM launcher}, and only when a run is handed over. A tool that exports them for the launcher and then runs the executable with \code{\verb~|--platform jvm~} gets them.}
\li{\strong{The cache directory is refused where a directory above it is open to others}: a checkout whose directories are group-writable (the development host's umask) is not a place for \code{RLVM_CACHE_DIR}; \code{\verb~|/tmp~} is, being sticky, and so is the default place, as long as neither the home directory nor its \code{\verb~|.cache~} can be written by the group or by others. In the arena-sized container of \src{bench/arena-docker.sh} the home directory lies inside the checkout, so a hand-over there needs \code{RLVM_CACHE_DIR}; without it every forced run falls back and says why.}
\li{\strong{The build's verification needs a place for a cache too}: it takes a new directory under \code{TMPDIR} or \code{\verb~|/tmp~}, and where that is not acceptable it logs that the hand-over was not exercised and goes on.}
\li{\strong{The arena definition needs no change}: its run line names no platform, a single pass is never handed over under \code{auto}, and the build line builds the same single file. \src{scripts/package.sh} runs the executable with an empty environment and passes unchanged.}
\li{\strong{One descriptor is inherited by the JVM} (section 4); \code{close_range} before \code{execv} would close it.}
\li{\strong{A word of the statistics}: with patch 5 a plain run ends its \code{\verb~|--stats~} line with \code{\verb~|platform=image why=forced~}, the word the rule has for "the platform is the image", also when nobody forced anything. It was left so that patch 5 stays one constant; \code{default} would be the better word there.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. What was not measured}

\ul{
\li{\strong{x86-64}, the arena's architecture: everything here is the aarch64 development host. The step itself (\code{execv} through the image's C interface, the file checks) has nothing of the architecture in it, but no x86-64 executable was built or run. The stock JDK of the arena's shell was not used either: the JDK of every row is Eclipse Temurin 25.0.5.1+2, and GraalVM CE 25 in three behaviour cases.}
\li{\strong{A quiet host.} Another wave of the team started while the windows ran: the cores outside the measurement set were at a load of 17 to 60 until midnight UTC and of 9 to 36 after it. The absolute times are 4 to 14 \% above the benchmark rows of the same build on a quiet host; the ratios between interleaved rounds are what the report rests on, and the one ratio that could be compared with a quiet-host row (the JVM's first pass against the executable on Mathlib, 1.11 there and 1.12 here) agrees.}
\li{\strong{An export larger than Mathlib}: there is none. The crossover of section 2.4 is an extrapolation.}
\li{\strong{A machine with less memory than the JVM's heap}: only as a container with a limit of 4 GB and a tutorial test (section 5.5). No corpus was checked under such a limit, and no machine of that size was used.}
\li{\strong{The reference engine and the strict policy on the handed-over JVM} as timings; they were run as behaviour cases on a tutorial test (the heap line of 13 GB is chosen).}
\li{\strong{A JDK newer than 25}, a JDK without HotSpot (it is refused by the file check), a JDK on a file system where a script cannot be executed.}
\li{\strong{What the AOT cache does to later passes} on this build: a window for it was queued and withdrawn when the queue of the host grew; the figure in section 3.6 is the earlier report's.}
\li{\strong{The AOT cache inside the hybrid}: it was measured through the JVM launcher. The hybrid does not record or use one (section 3.6 says why); \code{RLVM_JAVA_OPTS} can name one.}
\li{\strong{The Oracle GraalVM build} of the executable with the jars inside: the build script handles it the same way, by reading; it was not built.}
\li{\strong{The release workflow and the arena's build line from a clean copy} with the patched build script; the build script was run in a private copy of the tree with the published jars, and \src{scripts/package.sh} on its executable.}
\li{\strong{The probe's 30 s limit} (a java that hangs), and a hand-over when \code{\verb~|/proc~} is not mounted (the search for VM options and for the process's own user then fail, and the run stays in the executable; by reading).}
}
}
